   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "An improper integral is used to decide whether the series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{4n^{2}-1} \\) converges. Take f(x) to be the series' general term with n replaced by x, and the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         [
            "Ln",
            3
         ],
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{4x^{2}-1} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  4,
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "x",
                     2
                  ]
               ],
               -1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{4x^{2}-1} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{4x^{2}-1} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{2 x - 1}{2 x + 1} \\right)}}{4} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{4x^{2}-1} \\, dx = \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{2 b - 1}{2 b + 1} \\right)}}{4} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Ln",
                  [
                     "Divide",
                     [
                        "Add",
                        [
                           "Multiply",
                           2,
                           "b"
                        ],
                        -1
                     ],
                     [
                        "Add",
                        [
                           "Multiply",
                           2,
                           "b"
                        ],
                        1
                     ]
                  ]
               ],
               4
            ],
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Ln",
                  3
               ],
               4
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{4} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Ln",
               3
            ],
            4
         ]
      }
   ],
